WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.860
Scoping a project means deciding what problem it will solve and where its responsibility ends.

00:00:06.860 --> 00:00:10.672
Saying build a helpful assistant is hard to test.

00:00:10.672 --> 00:00:16.243
Saying answer questions about known orders gives you something specific to try.

00:00:16.243 --> 00:00:21.708
Architecture is a map of the parts and how information travels between them.

00:00:21.708 --> 00:00:29.733
For a small assistant you might draw the customer, the part that reads the question, and the system that holds the answer.

00:00:29.733 --> 00:00:33.014
Write down a success condition before building.

00:00:33.014 --> 00:00:37.519
Include an ordinary request and one outside the boundary.

00:00:37.519 --> 00:00:43.803
That way you can check both whether the assistant helps and whether it knows when to hand over.

00:00:43.803 --> 00:00:49.319
Scoping means being exact, so let's sort some real requests from Leo's queue.

00:00:49.319 --> 00:00:56.157
Questions like where is my order are Scout's core job: it looks the order up and tracks the parcel.

00:00:56.157 --> 00:01:03.006
Returns and exchanges are in too, because Scout can check the order, the policy and the stock.

00:01:03.006 --> 00:01:09.465
Refunds over two hundred dollars are half in: Scout starts them, and a person approves them.

00:01:09.465 --> 00:01:15.471
Product advice needs real gear expertise, so Scout hands that chat to a person.

00:01:15.471 --> 00:01:20.326
Marketing emails are not Scout's job at all, so it never tries.

00:01:20.326 --> 00:01:25.906
Writing down what Scout will not do matters just as much as what it will do.

00:01:25.906 --> 00:01:28.718
Orbit wants Scout to help with support.

00:01:28.718 --> 00:01:32.925
Start with one customer question: has my order shipped?

00:01:32.925 --> 00:01:35.906
Put order lookups inside the project.

00:01:35.906 --> 00:01:39.900
Put actions the shop has not authorized outside it.

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:46.609
If the customer asks for a change Scout may not make, a clear handoff is part of the design.

00:01:46.609 --> 00:01:49.804
Trace the ordinary question on a drawing.

00:01:49.804 --> 00:01:52.146
The customer supplies a number.

00:01:52.146 --> 00:01:55.838
The model identifies a need for an order lookup.

00:01:55.838 --> 00:02:02.634
A tool asks the records system, and the returned status gives the model a fact it can explain.

00:02:02.634 --> 00:02:07.948
Each connection has a purpose you can describe without knowing the code.

00:02:07.948 --> 00:02:12.313
Next, decide how you will know Scout works, before you build it.

00:02:12.313 --> 00:02:19.463
First, right answers: on a set of real test questions, Scout should pass 90 percent or more.

00:02:19.463 --> 00:02:27.882
Second, policy kept: no refund over two hundred dollars without a person, and no promise the policy doesn't make.

00:02:27.882 --> 00:02:35.280
Third, nothing invented: when a tool fails or data is missing, Scout says so instead of guessing.

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:42.680
The last two targets are zero, because one broken promise costs more than ten clumsy replies.

00:02:42.680 --> 00:02:46.363
Imagine two notes arrive on the project desk.

00:02:46.363 --> 00:02:49.441
The first asks whether Ana's tent has shipped.

00:02:49.441 --> 00:02:55.716
Your team has an order record, a lookup tool and a way to compare the reply with the record.

00:02:55.716 --> 00:02:58.772
This job has a testable boundary.

00:02:58.772 --> 00:03:02.905
The second asks which equipment is right for an expedition.

00:03:02.905 --> 00:03:10.106
A friendly paragraph would be easy to produce, but the order lookup provides no evidence for that advice.

00:03:10.106 --> 00:03:14.426
Route the question to a colleague with the relevant expertise.

00:03:14.426 --> 00:03:19.463
Write both notes into your acceptance test before you build the chat screen.

00:03:19.463 --> 00:03:23.180
The first passes when the status matches the record.

00:03:23.180 --> 00:03:27.364
The second passes when Scout recognises the boundary.

00:03:27.364 --> 00:03:31.760
A correct handoff can be a successful project outcome.

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:33.622
Now test the drawing.

00:03:33.622 --> 00:03:39.277
Use a known order marked shipped and check for that exact status in the reply.

00:03:39.277 --> 00:03:46.521
Use an order the system cannot find and check that Scout asks for help instead of inventing a result.

00:03:46.521 --> 00:03:53.144
Those two outcomes tell your team more about project scope than a list of attractive features.
